task: dataflow-failure
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-failure
worktree: ../okay-wt-dff
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 5

why: stage 4b ends with the sentence "a worker that dies takes the run
  with it". On one machine that is a bug; across four it is the normal
  case, and an engine that cannot survive it is a demo.

what: a partition whose worker dies is recomputed on a survivor, and
  the answer does not move. That is nearly free here and the reason is
  structural: a partition is a THUNK and its partial is a pure
  function of (parameters, index, count, bounds) — the same four
  things every worker is given. There is no lineage graph to walk and
  no checkpoint to restore, because nothing was mutated.

  The rule about WHICH failures retry, stated rather than discovered:
  a thrown transport error is a dead worker and retries on a
  survivor; a `Resp.Failed` is the worker's considered answer — it
  decoded the request and refused — and every worker runs the same
  build, so retrying it produces the identical refusal. It is
  returned, not retried.

  `Run` gains `retried`, so a suite can assert that recovery HAPPENED
  rather than infer it from the answer being right.

  Failure injection is SEEDED: which attempt on which worker fails is
  drawn from a seed, so a sweep over seeds explores many schedules and
  a red one names the seed that produced it. Not luck wearing a lab
  coat.

  And the headline: a real worker PROCESS killed mid-run, with the
  job finishing anyway.

what this is NOT: exactly-once execution. A worker that dies after
  computing but before its reply arrives has its partition computed
  twice, and that is correct because the coordinator keeps exactly
  one partial per partition — exactly-once OUTCOME, which is the same
  words specs/persist.md already settled on.

predict: the retry itself will be twenty lines and the interesting
  half will be the ROTATION — picking a survivor without two fibres
  racing on the live set, and making "no workers left" name the
  original failure rather than an empty-collection error.
